(vector (map (:adr/authoritative_for (vector "ISCO-08 3354 (Government Licensing Officials) actor coverage" "kotoba.occupation registry entry for \"3354\"")), (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:actor/wave 2), (:adr/supersedes (vector)), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/scope-excluded-ops (vector :issue-license :deny-license-application :revoke-license :renew-license :grant-license :approve-license-application :suspend-license :reject-license-application)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/superseded_by (vector)), (:adr/topic "cloud-itonami-isco-3354-government-licensing-officials-coverage"), (:adr/related (vector "adr-2607121000-cloud-itonami-global-isic-isco-reverse-toposort-plan" "adr-2607012000-cloud-itonami-isco-occupation-blueprints" "adr-2700003341-cloud-itonami-isco-3341-office-supervisors-coverage" "adr-2790003342-cloud-itonami-isco-3342-legal-secretaries-coverage" "adr-2790003344-cloud-itonami-isco-3344-medical-secretaries-coverage")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "issue the license" "issue a license" "grant the license" "grant a license" "deny the license application" "reject the license application" "revoke the license" "revoke a license" "suspend the license" "renew the license")), (:adr/id 2791003354), (:actor/domain :government/licensing-coordination), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-application-record :schedule-review-appointment :flag-licensing-review :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :office-provenance :effect-propose :closed-op-allowlist :application-basis :finalization-language)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "3354"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-licensing-review)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR-2791003354: cloud-itonami-isco-3354 — Independent Government Licensing Coordination Practice\n\n**Status**: accepted\n**Date**: 2026-07-17\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki (+ Claude, standing authorization per CLAUDE.md)\n**Scope**: `kotoba-lang/occupation`, `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3354`\n**Builds on**: ADR-2607121000 (cloud-itonami global ISIC/ISCO reverse-toposort\nrollout plan), ADR-2607012000 (cloud-itonami ISCO occupation blueprints)\n**Related**: `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313` (Accounting Associate\nProfessionals — the named structural reference, read in full: `store`/\n`advisor`/`governor`/`actor` module shape, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph,\nfull doc set), `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3341` (Office Supervisors\n— the closer structural analog also read in full and used as the direct\ncopy template, itself explicitly modeled on 3313's module shape: same\nclosed four-op coordination-only allowlist shape with one always-escalate\nflag op and one cost-ceiling escalate supply-order op), ADR-2790003342/\nADR-2790003344 (concurrent sibling ISCO-track coordination-only actors in\nthe same batch, cross-checked for the self-tripping-bug guardrail\nconvention)\n\n## Context\n\nISCO-08 3354 (Government Licensing Officials) was a `:spec`-only entry in\n`kotoba-lang/occupation`'s registry (`resources/kotoba/occupation/\nregistry.edn`) with no `:repo`/`:business-id`. Identity independently\nverified against a fresh clone of `kotoba-lang/occupation` before any work\nbegan, via `clojure -M -e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println\n(kotoba.occupation/get-occupation \\\"3354\\\"))\"` (not hand-parsing the\ntx-data blob): returned `{:id 3354, :name Government Licensing Officials,\n:maturity :spec, :required-technologies [:robotics :identity\n:audit-ledger], :optional-technologies [], :operating-states [:intake\n:propose :approve :execute :audit]}` — name matches \"Government Licensing\nOfficials\" exactly, no mismatch found. No prior `cloud-itonami-isco-3354`\nGitHub repository existed at the `cloud-itonami` org (404 confirmed via\n`gh api` before any work began).\n\n**Domain / licensing-authority dimension — the hardest guardrail in this\nbatch.** Government Licensing Officials issue, renew, deny and revoke\nlicenses and permits (business licenses, professional licenses, etc.) —\ndecisions that determine whether a person or business may legally operate.\nPer explicit owner guidance, this actor must NEVER be designed to\nexercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising ANY licensing/\npermitting-decision authority: it is a documentation/logistics-\ncoordination robot ONLY. Unlike a typical \"gate a risky action behind\nhuman escalation\" design, the guidance required that no op resembling\nissuing, denying or revoking a license exist ANYWHERE in the closed\nop-allowlist at all — structurally absent, not merely gated. Any\nobservation suggesting a licensing decision is needed is surfaced ONLY via\nan always-escalate `:flag-licensing-review` op that a human licensing\nofficial reviews and decides.\n\n**Scope**: coordination only, mirrored closely on the verified reference\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313` (Accounting Associate\nProfessionals) module shape (`langgraph.graph`-based actor: `:intake ->\n:advise -> :govern -> :decide -+-> :commit +-> :request-approval +->\n:hold`, Advisor/Governor/Store split, in-memory `MemStore` Store protocol,\n`mock-advisor`/`llm-advisor` seam), via the closer structural analog\n`cloud-itonami-isco-3341` (Office Supervisors, itself explicitly modeled\non 3313 in its own docstrings) as the direct copy template — 3341's\nclosed four-op allowlist shape (one log op, one schedule op, one\nalways-escalate flag op, one cost-ceiling-escalate supply-order op) maps\ncleanly onto the licensing-coordination domain. Domain-adapted:\n`govlicensing.*` namespace in place of `officesupervision.*`, an\noffice/application (license-application case-file) entity pair in place\nof an office/staff-member entity pair, application-record-logging/\nreview-appointment-scheduling/supply-order ops in place of workflow-\nlogging/staff-scheduling/supply-order ops, and `:flag-licensing-review` in\nplace of `:flag-hr-concern`.\n\n## Decision\n\n### 1. Closed proposal-op allowlist, all `:effect :propose` (structurally excludes any licensing-decision op)\n\n- `:log-application-record` — license-application intake/status data\n  entry (documentation only, not a decision); requires a REGISTERED\n  application case file belonging to the requesting office\n- `:schedule-review-appointment` — licensing-review/inspection\n  appointment scheduling proposal; requires a REGISTERED application\n- `:flag-licensing-review` — surface an application that needs human\n  licensing-official review (new application, renewal, compliance-issue\n  trigger); requires a REGISTERED application; **ALWAYS escalates**,\n  never auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` — office-equipment procurement proposal;\n  checked against the office's registered `:max-supply-cost` ceiling\n  (over-ceiling always escalates)\n\nNo op that issues, denies, renews or revokes a license or permit is a\nmember of this set — not `:issue-license`, not `:deny-license-application`,\nnot `:revoke-license`, not `:renew-license`, not any synonym. These are\nnot merely excluded by a denylist check; they simply do not exist\nanywhere in `govlicensing.governor`'s `allowed-ops` set, in\n`govlicensing.advisor`'s `rationale-for` case dispatch, or in any other\nnamespace in the repository — grep-verifiable structural absence. An op\noutside the closed allowlist is a hard, permanent, non-overridable block\n(`:op-not-allowed`), regardless of confidence or human approval.\n\n### 2. Governor rules: five HARD checks (permanent, un-overridable), plus escalation\n\n1. **Office unregistered** — the requesting government licensing office\n   must be independently verified/registered in the store before ANY\n   action.\n2. **Effect not `:propose`** — any proposal whose `:effect` is not\n   `:propose` is, by construction, a claim to directly actuate outside\n   governance; the governor never dispatches hardware and never commits\n   an operating record itself.\n3. **Closed op allowlist** — `:op` must be one of the four ops above.\n4. **Application basis** — `:log-application-record`,\n   `:schedule-review-appointment` and `:flag-licensing-review` must cite\n   a REGISTERED license-application case file belonging to the\n   requesting office (`:unknown-application` / `:application-wrong-office`);\n   this actor never opens a case file ex nihilo, it only coordinates\n   around one a human intake process has already registered.\n5. **Finalization-language (defense-in-depth)** — the proposal's\n   free-text `:rationale` must not contain a licensing-decision\n   ACTION-phrase (\"issue the license\", \"deny the license application\",\n   \"revoke the license\", \"renew the license\", \"grant a license\", etc.) —\n   never a bare noun like \"license\"/\"permit\" alone, which would\n   false-positive on entirely ordinary rationale text (e.g.\n   `:flag-licensing-review`'s own default rationale, which necessarily\n   discusses licenses/permits while disclaiming any decision authority\n   over them). This catches an *allowed* op (e.g.\n   `:log-application-record`) reused to smuggle a licensing decision\n   through free text, independent of check 3's exact-keyword match, and\n   fires regardless of which `:op` it is nominally filed under.\n\n**Known self-tripping bug class, guarded against by a dedicated\nregression test.** This fleet has repeatedly discovered the same bug\nclass on other coordination-only verticals: a governor scope-exclusion\nterm list phrased as a bare noun can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale/disclaimer text for a legitimate, allowed\nproposal, causing the actor to self-block on its own happy path.\n`govlicensing.governor`'s `finalization-phrases` are deliberately phrased\nas multi-word article+verb+object action-phrases (\"issue the license\",\n\"deny the license application\", \"revoke the license\" — never a bare noun\nsuch as \"license\"/\"permit\" in isolation), and `govlicensing.advisor`'s\n`:flag-licensing-review` default rationale is deliberately worded with\nplural/third-person verb forms (\"never issues, denies, renews or revokes\nany license or permit\") that share no contiguous substring with any\nfinalization phrase — verified, not assumed: a dedicated test,\n`never-self-trips-on-default-mock-advisor-proposals` in `governor_test.clj`,\nasserts every default mock-advisor proposal for all four allowed ops\n(including three different `:flag-licensing-review` concern-category\nvariants) clears the governor with `:finalization-language-blocked`\nabsent from `:violations`. A companion true-positive test,\n`hard-on-finalization-language-regardless-of-op`, confirms five\nhand-crafted licensing-decision rationales (issue/deny/revoke/renew/grant)\ndo still hard-block when actually present. A third test,\n`hard-on-op-not-in-closed-allowlist`, confirms eight synthetic\nlicensing-decision op keywords (`:issue-license`,\n`:deny-license-application`, `:revoke-license`, `:renew-license`,\n`:grant-license`, `:approve-license-application`, `:suspend-license`,\n`:reject-license-application`) are all rejected as `:op-not-allowed` if a\nrogue advisor ever proposed them — proving the closed-allowlist gate\nholds even if the structural-absence guarantee were somehow bypassed\nupstream.\n\nEscalation (SOFT, always human sign-off, only reached when the governor is\notherwise clean):\n\n- `:flag-licensing-review` — always, regardless of confidence;\n  structurally never a member of any auto-commit path\n  (`always-escalate-ops` unconditionally includes it).\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` above the office's registered\n  `:max-supply-cost` — always, regardless of confidence.\n- Low advisor confidence (`< governor/confidence-floor` = 0.6).\n\n### 3. Module shape\n\n`govlicensing.store` (MemStore, string-keyed `offices`/`applications`\ndirectories, append-only audit ledger), `govlicensing.advisor` (Licensing\nCoordination Advisor, mock + a real-LLM seam whose system prompt\nexplicitly states the actor has NO licensing/permitting-decision\nauthority), `govlicensing.governor` (LicensingCoordinationGovernor),\n`govlicensing.actor` (the `langgraph.graph` StateGraph: intake → advise →\ngovern → decide → commit | hold | request-approval). All source is\nportable `.cljc` with no JVM-only interop anywhere in `src/` (mock-only\nadvisor, cljs-compatible; test files are `.clj`, matching the verified\nreference exactly). `deps.edn` mirrors the reference exactly: only\n`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph` pinned at the same `:git/sha`, no\n`kotoba-lang/technology` dependency (that sibling dependency is only\nneeded inside the `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry checkout itself, not\nin individual actor repos — confirmed by prior sibling sessions this\nbatch and reconfirmed by this session's own `deps.edn` diff against the\nreference).\n\nFull doc set scaffolded matching the reference: `README.md` (with a\ndedicated \"No licensing-decision authority\" section spelling out the\nstructural-absence guarantee), `docs/business-model.md`,\n`docs/operator-guide.md` (explicitly states no code path, configuration or\noverride may allow the actor to issue/deny/renew/revoke a license — this\nmust remain structurally impossible, not merely disabled by\nconfiguration), `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `GOVERNANCE.md`,\n`SECURITY.md`, `LICENSE` (AGPL-3.0-or-later, copied verbatim from the\nreference, 661 lines).\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Registry entry updated in place via a byte-verified targeted string\n  replace of the existing `{:id \"3354\", :name \"Government Licensing\n  Officials\", ...}` block only (prefix/suffix of the surrounding tx-data\n  blob confirmed byte-identical before/after via a first-diff/last-diff\n  scan, no adjacent entry touched): `:name` / `:required-technologies` /\n  `:optional-technologies` / `:operating-states` left exactly as-is\n  (matching sibling `:implemented` entries' convention of keeping the\n  registry's generic `[:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit]` shape\n  rather than the actor's literal StateGraph node names), `:maturity`\n  `:spec` → `:implemented`, `:repo`\n  `https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3354` and\n  `:business-id \"cloud-itonami-isco-3354\"` added. Landed via the GitHub\n  Contents API with blob-SHA CAS on the first attempt (no 409): commit\n  `370e8f2784c405017053e67d16454243b701e553`.\n- Actor repo `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3354` scaffolded (fresh —\n  no prior repository existed, 404 confirmed before any work began) and\n  pushed to `main`: commit `d0ff121fc61e12710395c90854715cba8755575d`.\n  Test suite run on a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed actor repo:\n  **`Ran 18 tests containing 68 assertions. 0 failures, 0 errors.`**\n  (`clojure -M:test`). No mojibake found in the Japanese-language governor\n  violation text (`未登録`, `専管事項`, `恒久的` all confirmed intact byte-for-byte).\n- `test/kotoba/occupation_test.clj`'s `maturity-summary` numeric\n  assertions and a new dedicated `government-licensing-officials-3354-\n  implemented` deftest landed via three successive CAS rounds, because\n  this was one of six ISCO-track sessions landing concurrently in the same\n  batch window and the shared registry/test files kept drifting between\n  fetch and PUT: round 1 (`:spec` 215 → 214, `:implemented` 221 → 222,\n  commit `2401e90e59c3a019351bde1556cc353a2d49bc61`) went stale\n  immediately (a sibling promotion landed `214 → 213` /\n  `222 → 223` before this session's next fresh-clone verification); round\n  2 re-synced to the live numbers (commit\n  `9bc74524083b8562cb956f6de36f62dd9332a5ea`) and also went stale the same\n  way (`213 → 212` / `223 → 224`); round 3 re-synced again (commit\n  `20db636e9f460b0a62ea22dc35ceebe206cf26a3`). A final fresh re-clone\n  after round 3 showed the shared file had, in the interim, been\n  independently re-synced once more by another concurrent sibling session\n  to `211`/`225` (`436` total unchanged) — already green, this session's\n  own dedicated `government-licensing-officials-3354-implemented` test and\n  ADR-reference comment intact and unclobbered. This session did not chase\n  the counter past that point: the file was live-verified green via a\n  fresh clone, which is the actual correctness bar (a fixed number would\n  have gone stale again within minutes regardless of how many more rounds\n  were run).\n- Final post-merge re-verification of the registry (brand-new scratch\n  directory, fresh `kotoba-lang/occupation` clone plus a fresh\n  `kotoba-lang/technology` sibling at the correct `../technology` relative\n  path): `clojure -M:test` re-run — **`Ran 17 tests containing 957\n  assertions. 0 failures, 0 errors.`**; `(kotoba.occupation/get-occupation\n  \"3354\")` confirmed `:maturity :implemented` with the correct\n  `:repo`/`:business-id`; `(kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary)` confirmed\n  `{:total 436, :spec 211, :blueprint 0, :implemented 225}` at time of\n  this ADR's landing (expected to keep moving as the concurrent batch\n  continues — future readers should recompute live rather than trust this\n  snapshot); `registry.edn` and `occupation_test.clj` both scanned and\n  confirmed valid UTF-8 with zero Unicode replacement-character\n  occurrences (no mojibake).\n\n## Notes for future ISCO-track agents (observed live this session)\n\n- The named reference `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` (Accounting Associate\n  Professionals) does not itself carry a closed-op-allowlist /\n  finalization-language-block pattern — its domain (bookkeeping) needed\n  an arithmetic ceiling + source-document-attached check instead, no\n  \"scope of decision authority\" exclusion. The closed-allowlist +\n  finalization-language pattern used here was introduced by later\n  sibling actors in the coordination-only lineage (3341/3342/3343/3344/\n  3333), all of which explicitly say \"Modeled on cloud-itonami-isco-3313's\n  ...\" in their own docstrings — i.e. they are a faithful, verified\n  evolution of the 3313 module shape, not a divergent pattern. A future\n  agent building a coordination-only actor with a hard scope-of-authority\n  exclusion should read one of the coordination-only siblings (this repo,\n  or 3341/3342/3343/3344) directly rather than 3313 alone, even though\n  3313 remains the named canonical reference for the base module shape\n  (Store/Advisor/Governor/Actor split, StateGraph topology, doc set).\n- Confirms prior sibling sessions' finding: `kotoba-lang/occupation`'s own\n  `deps.edn` declares `io.github.kotoba-lang/technology {:local/root\n  \"../technology\"}`, but no individual `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor repo\n  depends on `kotoba-lang/technology` — that sibling dependency is only\n  needed when working inside the `kotoba-lang/occupation` checkout itself.\n- The GitHub Contents API PUT-with-CAS discipline for `registry.edn`\n  worked on the first attempt this session (no 409) for the registry\n  itself, but `occupation_test.clj` needed three rounds purely because of\n  concurrent-sibling write races on that specific file — confirms this\n  batch's warning that `occupation_test.clj` is the hot-contention file,\n  not `registry.edn`. Always re-fetch the blob SHA immediately before each\n  PUT attempt on `occupation_test.clj`, and always re-verify with a fresh\n  clone afterward rather than trusting the PUT response alone — a\n  successful PUT only proves this session's own edit landed cleanly, not\n  that a later sibling PUT hasn't already superseded the numbers again.\n- `gh api repos/<org>/<repo>/contents/<path>` payloads for these\n  ~60-130KB files were built as explicit JSON (`message`/`content`/`sha`/\n  `branch` as real JSON string fields via a Python-written payload file)\n  and sent via `gh api --method PUT ... --input <payload.json>`, avoiding\n  the `-f content=@file` convenience syntax that a prior sibling session\n  reported failing with `422 content is not valid Base64` for large\n  payloads.\n\n## References\n\n- `cloud-itonami-isco-3313/` (named reference, read in full — verified\n  working base module shape: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor`,\n  `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set; its own registry `:maturity`\n  is `:implemented`)\n- `cloud-itonami-isco-3341/` (closer structural analog, read in full —\n  direct copy template for the closed four-op coordination-only allowlist\n  shape)\n- ADR-2790003342 (`cloud-itonami-isco-3342`), ADR-2790003344\n  (`cloud-itonami-isco-3344`) — concurrent ISCO-track siblings in the same\n  batch, same coordination-only shape, cross-checked for the\n  self-tripping-bug guardrail convention\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` `resources/kotoba/occupation/registry.edn`\n  `\"3354\"` entry\n- ADR-2607121000 (Wave definition, reverse-toposort rollout plan — ISCO\n  Wave 2, sub-major 33/35 coordination)\n"), (:adr/date "2026-07-17"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order office/:max-supply-cost}"), (:test/counts "{:total 18, :assertions 68, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Independent Government Licensing Coordination Practice"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :closed-allowlist-synthetic-op-regression)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-3354"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3354"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3354)), (:actor/auto-commit-ops (vector :log-application-record :schedule-review-appointment :coordinate-supply-order)), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-3354 -- Independent Government Licensing Coordination Practice"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000 2790003342 2790003344)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-3354)))